wintypes: resolve Windows Runtime type metadata, so .NET can bind WinRT types

Power BI Desktop is a .NET Framework application and its DI container builds a view whose type initialiser asks
.NET for WinRT types:

  Microsoft.PowerBI.Client.Windows.Services.StoragePropertiesProvider..cctor
    RequiredTypes = { typeof(StorageFile).FullName, typeof(StorageItemContentProperties).FullName }

.NET Framework resolves such a name by asking the OS where the type's metadata lives. Wine's DLL for that,
wintypes.dll - the same DLL Windows uses, measured: LoadLibraryW("api-ms-win-ro-typeresolution-l1-1-1.dll") on
Windows 10 build 26200 answers C:\WINDOWS\SYSTEM32\wintypes.dll - only had stub plumbing: RoResolveNamespace
returned RO_E_METADATA_NAME_NOT_FOUND unconditionally and RoGetMetaDataFile was an unimplemented spec stub. The
result was a hard failure before any window exists:

  System.TypeInitializationException: The type initializer for 'StoragePropertiesProvider' threw an exception
  System.TypeLoadException: Could not find Windows Runtime type 'Windows.Storage.StorageFile'
  -> Failed to resolve type: Microsoft.PowerBI.Client.Windows.Views.ReportView   (DI scope: App/MainForm)

On Windows this works because the OS answers both calls from the WinRT metadata it ships in
%SystemRoot%\System32\WinMetadata\*.winmd, which is what this patch implements, with the behaviour measured on
Windows (tools/winapi/roprobe.exe):

  RoResolveNamespace("Windows.Storage")  -> S_OK, 1 file  ...\Windows.Storage.winmd, 0 sub-namespaces
  RoResolveNamespace("Windows")          -> S_OK, 0 files, 18 sub-namespaces (AI, ApplicationModel, Data, ...)
  RoResolveNamespace("")                 -> E_INVALIDARG
  RoGetMetaDataFile("Windows.Storage.StorageFile") -> S_OK, path=...\Windows.Storage.winmd, token=0x02000138
  RoGetMetaDataFile("Windows.Storage")             -> RO_E_METADATA_NAME_IS_NAMESPACE      (0x80000010)
  RoGetMetaDataFile("Windows.Nonexistent.Type")    -> RO_E_METADATA_NAME_NOT_FOUND        (0x8000000f)
  RoGetMetaDataFile("Windows.Storage.")            -> RO_E_METADATA_INVALID_TYPE_FORMAT   (0x80000011)

So a metadata file is returned when its own name is a namespace prefix of the requested namespace (and no file for
"Windows", which none is named after), the sub-namespaces are the ones that have a metadata file of their own,
named relative to the namespace (18 for "Windows", none for "Windows.Storage"), and a type resolves to the file
named after its namespace and, for the type of an API contract, to the contract file that describes the contract.
The namespace and type lookups read Wine's own rometadata.dll (a port of the library Windows uses), which
wintypes now imports; the ApiInformation presence queries answer from what Wine can provide, so that a program
degrades gracefully (a type is present when Wine can activate it, so Windows.Storage.StorageFile is reported
absent - it is metadata Wine describes but does not implement - and a method or enum value is present when the
metadata of an activatable type declares it).

Known gap: Wine's rometadata.dll implements IMetaDataImport but not IMetaDataImport2 (that vtable repeats the 65
IMetaDataImport methods and adds 8), so RoGetMetaDataFile returns a reader only when the dispenser it was given
has that interface; with Wine's own dispenser the call answers E_NOINTERFACE (0x80004002) and resets its outputs
as the API contract requires, with the resolved path in the warning. clr.dll passes its own dispenser and never
calls RoGetMetaDataFile - it resolves WinRT types through RoResolveNamespace alone - so the app path is
unaffected. Closing it is a dlls/rometadata change.

Verified in prefix/burn48 and prefix/pbi2 (evidence in logs/winrt/, docs/WINRT_METADATA.md): the net48 probe
(tools/winrt/probe) went from four "TypeLoadException: Could not find Windows Runtime type" to OK for
Windows.Storage.StorageFile, Windows.Storage.StorageFolder, Windows.Storage.FileProperties.BasicProperties and
Windows.Foundation.Metadata.ApiInformation (failures=4 -> 0, rc=1 -> 0); the native probe matches the Windows
reference except for the path letter case and the reader gap above; and Power BI opens the user's report with
RoResolveNamespace answering "1 metadata files" for Windows.Storage / Windows.Storage.FileProperties /
Windows.Foundation, no "Failed to resolve type" dialog, no unhandled exception and a complete startup chain in
its own trace (Create Splash, MainForm/ctor end, ActivateMainWindow end, Main Window Loaded end).
